<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Cut the Paper.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of written instructions for classroom activities. Reading: Demonstrate comprehension of basic descriptions in texts. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of written instructions for classroom activities by following them step-by-step (e.g., "I can cut and fold the paper."). Can demonstrate comprehension of basic descriptions in texts by summarizing key details from reading materials (e.g., "The flowchart is colorful."). Can recognize and divide compound and multi-syllabic words into syllables (e.g., "cardboard" into "card-board" and "instructional" into "in-struc-tion-al").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher holds up various classroom craft materials (e.g., paper, scissors, glue, crayons, cardboard) and asks students, "What are these?" and "What can we make with these?" The teacher displays simple pictures of crafts like a paper mask or a card. Students identify the objects and share ideas. The teacher writes key vocabulary words from Lesson 1 on the board as students recall them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the written vocabulary words on the board (e.g., "scissors," "paper," "glue") and models reading them clearly, emphasizing the pronunciation of /s/ in "scissors" and /p/ in "paper." The teacher then writes a simple imperative like "Cut the paper." and models reading it aloud, pointing to each word as they read, and performing the action.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses at least two Concept Checking Questions (CCQs) and examples to ensure understanding.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "scissors" written on the board: "Is this for drawing or cutting?" (Expected answer: "Cutting.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the written instruction "Fold the paper.": "Do I throw the paper away or bend it?" (Expected answer: "Bend it.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Following Instructions at School")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful flowchart on a poster or projector titled "How to Make a Paper Card." The flowchart contains 4-5 simple, illustrated steps with written instructions using target vocabulary (e.g., "First, fold the paper. Next, cut a small circle. Then, paste the circle on the card. Finally, draw a face."). The genre is instructional text, register is informal, and the format is a visual flowchart.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Interpreting simple written instructions and basic descriptions in a flowchart format to understand how to complete a task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students look at the flowchart. Teacher asks: "What are we going to make?" (Expected answer: "A card.") "Is the flowchart colorful or small?" (Expected answer: "Colorful.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Reading - True/False:</b> Teacher reads statements, and students say "True" or "False" based on the flowchart. Examples: "First, you draw a face." (False) "You need glue to paste." (True) "You cut the paper next." (True/False depending on flowchart example).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching Pictures to Instructions:</b> The teacher provides individual strips of paper with the written instructions from the flowchart (e.g., "Fold the paper," "Cut a small circle," "Paste the circle," "Draw a face"). Students match these written instructions to corresponding pictures of the steps displayed separately.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading written instructions with sequencing words (first, next, then, finally) and plural imperatives ("Let's make a poster."), identifying target vocabulary (paper, scissors, glue, paste, cut, fold, draw, colorful), and practicing phonemic awareness (recognizing and dividing compound words and multi-syllabic words).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Sequencing and Vocabulary Match:</b> Students receive a handout with incomplete instructions from the flowchart (e.g., "___, fold the paper. ___, cut a small circle."). They read the sentences and fill in the blanks with the correct sequencing words (first, next, then, finally) from a word bank. Additionally, they match target vocabulary words (e.g., "scissors," "glue," "colorful") to their corresponding pictures or short descriptions on the same handout.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Reordering & Phonemic Awareness:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read a set of jumbled instruction sentences (e.g., "the / a / paste / circle." "paper / cut / the.") and rewrite them in the correct order to form complete instructions.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher writes compound words like "cardboard," "classroom," "flowchart" and multi-syllabic words like "instructional," "decorate" on the board. Students read these words. Then, in pairs, they read the words and draw lines to divide them into syllables (e.g., card-board, class-room, flow-chart, in-struc-tion-al, dec-o-rate), reinforcing grapheme-phoneme decoding.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Read and Follow (Mental):</b> In pairs, one student (A) reads a simple two-step instruction (e.g., "First, draw a big square. Next, cut the square.") from a provided list. Their partner (B) listens and mentally visualizes or mimes the action. Then, Partner B describes the steps back to Partner A to check comprehension, without actually performing the task. They switch roles.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Following Instructions at School")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4) to collaboratively follow a new set of written instructions to create a simple paper craft (e.g., a "paper mask" or a "decoration"). The instructions are presented as a simple step-by-step list with accompanying pictures. Each group receives a material kit (paper, scissors, glue, crayons/markers). Students must read the instructions aloud within their group and follow them to complete the craft. One student can read a step, and the others follow. They can use pragmatic expressions like, "What do I need to do next?" if they are unsure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly simulates following instructions for an art project in the classroom, reinforcing the theme "First, I Cut the Paper." and the scenario "Following Instructions at School." Students demonstrate they "can show comprehension of written instructions for classroom activities by following them step-by-step."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in interpreting written instructions, collaborative interaction using imperatives ("Let's cut the paper!") and sequencing words ("First, next"), and successful completion of the craft based on reading comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist. The teacher circulates among the groups during Stage 4, noting students' ability to read, interpret, and follow instructions. The checklist includes criteria such as "Reads instructions accurately," "Follows steps in order," "Identifies necessary materials (nouns)," "Recognizes sequencing words," and "Participates in group work."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The completed paper craft (mask or decoration) from Stage 4 will serve as evidence of comprehension. Students will also orally summarize one step they followed, stating "First, I cut the paper" or "Next, I will paste the glue."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of written instructions by correctly following the steps to complete the craft.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (e.g., "paper," "scissors," "glue," "cut," "fold," "paste") when discussing the process.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes and applies sequencing words (first, next) from the instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes compound words and divides them into syllables (e.g., "card-board").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can read and follow instructions for making a (mask/decoration). Yes / No"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One word I learned to divide into syllables today was: _________."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading instructions was easy for me today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading instructions was difficult, and what can I do to improve?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The reading activities effectively engaged students in understanding sequential instructions. The visual support of the flowchart and pictures was crucial for A1.3 level learners. Phonemic awareness practice on compound words helped with decoding longer words. Some students still struggled with distinguishing "then" and "next" in written instructions; this will require more explicit practice. I need to prepare more varied texts with simple descriptions in the next lesson to build on their comprehension of basic details, and continue scaffolding their ability to follow written instructions. For the next lesson, we will transition from reading instructions to giving and receiving them orally, focusing on speaking and interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This reading comprehension lesson on following instructions will lead into Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), where students will practice giving instructions to their peers using the same sequencing words and vocabulary, building on their understanding from reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>